# Syntax highlighting

Syntax highlighting (SPEC.md §5.5). A code chunk is not a valid program — it is
target-language text with chunk-use holes spliced in, and a hole is lit's syntax,
not the target's. So the tokenizer never sees a hole: tree-sitter gets only the
literal runs between holes, and each hole's anchor is spliced back afterward — the
same discipline the math pass uses (§6.2). Color is a CSS `tok-*` class, never
injected text, so copy-paste (§5.4) and the annotation layer's quote selectors
(§9.2) see the same bytes as before, and there is no script (§6.3).

The module is the language enum and its resolver, the highlight-name tables, a
per-language grammar cache, and the line-splitting merge that does the real work.

The banner doc-comment below shows a hole example. In a code chunk a bare hole is
a chunk use, so each bracket pair carries the noweb at-escape; the example survives
tangle as literal text and never expands.
